## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| `Object.create(null)` as accumulator | Produces a **prototype-free** object, so keys like `"constructor"` or `"toString"` never shadow inherited properties |
| `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(…)` | Safe existence check that works even on null-prototype objects |
| Guard clauses with `TypeError` | Fails fast with a clear message rather than silently misbehaving |
| Items stored **by reference** | Avoids unexpected deep-cloning costs; callers mutate items at their own discretion |
| Generic `T` / `K` type parameters | Fully type-safe in TypeScript without any `any` casts |

# `groupBy` Utility Function

## Implementation

```javascript
/**
 * Groups an array of items into an object (or Map) where each key maps to an
 * array of items that share that key, as determined by a user-supplied key
 * function.
 *
 * @template T        - The type of elements in the input array.
 * @template K        - The type of the derived key (string | number | symbol).
 *
 * @param {T[]}            arr   - The array of items to group.
 *                                 • Passing an empty array returns `{}`.
 *                                 • The original array is never mutated.
 * @param {(item: T, index: number, array: T[]) => K} keyFn
 *                                 A function called for every element that
 *                                 returns the key under which that element
 *                                 should be grouped.
 *                                 • Receives the same three arguments as
 *                                   `Array.prototype.map` callbacks.
 *                                 • Returning `null` or `undefined` is allowed;
 *                                   those values are coerced to the strings
 *                                   `"null"` / `"undefined"` when used as
 *                                   object keys (standard JS behaviour).
 *
 * @returns {{ [key: string]: T[] }}
 *                                 A plain object whose own enumerable
 *                                 properties are the distinct keys produced by
 *                                 `keyFn`, each holding an array of items
 *                                 (in their original order) that mapped to
 *                                 that key.
 *
 * @throws {TypeError} If `arr` is not an array.
 * @throws {TypeError} If `keyFn` is not a function.
 *
 * @example
 * // ── Basic grouping by a string property ──────────────────────────────────
 * const pets = [
 *   { name: 'Luna',   species: 'cat' },
 *   { name: 'Max',    species: 'dog' },
 *   { name: 'Bella',  species: 'cat' },
 *   { name: 'Buddy',  species: 'dog' },
 *   { name: 'Nemo',   species: 'fish' },
 * ];
 *
 * groupBy(pets, pet => pet.species);
 * // {
 * //   cat:  [{ name: 'Luna', … }, { name: 'Bella', … }],
 * //   dog:  [{ name: 'Max',  … }, { name: 'Buddy', … }],
 * //   fish: [{ name: 'Nemo', … }],
 * // }
 *
 * @example
 * // ── Grouping numbers into buckets ────────────────────────────────────────
 * groupBy([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], n => (n % 2 === 0 ? 'even' : 'odd'));
 * // { odd: [1, 3, 5], even: [2, 4, 6] }
 *
 * @example
 * // ── Using the index argument ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 * groupBy(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], (_, i) => (i < 2 ? 'first-half' : 'second-half'));
 * // { 'first-half': ['a', 'b'], 'second-half': ['c', 'd'] }
 *
 * @example
 * // ── Derived / computed keys ───────────────────────────────────────────────
 * const words = ['one', 'two', 'three', 'four', 'five'];
 * groupBy(words, w => w.length);
 * // { '3': ['one', 'two'], '4': ['four', 'five'], '5': ['three'] }
 */
function groupBy(arr, keyFn) {
  // ── Guard clauses ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    throw new TypeError(`groupBy: expected an array as the first argument, got ${typeof arr}`);
  }
  if (typeof keyFn !== 'function') {
    throw new TypeError(`groupBy: expected a function as the second argument, got ${typeof keyFn}`);
  }

  // ── Core reduction ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  return arr.reduce((groups, item, index, array) => {
    const key = keyFn(item, index, array);

    // Initialise the bucket on first encounter, then push.
    if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(groups, key)) {
      groups[key] = [];
    }
    groups[key].push(item);

    return groups;
  }, Object.create(null)); // null prototype avoids collisions with built-ins
}
```

