### Design decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Lazy expiry** | Entries are only removed when accessed, avoiding background timers and keeping the class dependency-free. |
| **FIFO eviction** | `Map` preserves insertion order, so the first key returned by `.keys()` is always the oldest. |
| **Per-entry TTL** | Allows fine-grained control without needing a separate cache instance. |
| **Chaining from `set`** | Returns `this` so multiple `set` calls can be written fluently. |
| **`has` delegates to `get`** | Ensures expiry logic lives in exactly one place, preventing drift. |

## Cache Class

Here's a clean, well-documented `Cache` class with a straightforward public API:

```javascript
/**
 * A simple in-memory key-value cache with optional TTL (time-to-live) support.
 *
 * @example
 * const cache = new Cache();
 * cache.set("user:1", { name: "Alice" }, 5000);
 * cache.get("user:1"); // { name: "Alice" }
 * cache.has("user:1"); // true
 * cache.delete("user:1");
 */
class Cache {
  /** @type {Map<string, { value: unknown; expiresAt: number | null }>} */
  #store = new Map();

  /**
   * Retrieves a value from the cache by key.
   * Returns `undefined` if the key does not exist or has expired.
   *
   * @param {string} key - The key to look up.
   * @returns {unknown | undefined} The cached value, or `undefined` if not found.
   *
   * @example
   * cache.set("color", "blue");
   * cache.get("color"); // "blue"
   * cache.get("missing"); // undefined
   */
  get(key) {
    const entry = this.#store.get(key);

    if (!entry) return undefined;

    if (entry.expiresAt !== null && Date.now() > entry.expiresAt) {
      this.#store.delete(key);
      return undefined;
    }

    return entry.value;
  }

  /**
   * Stores a value in the cache under the given key.
   * An optional TTL (in milliseconds) controls how long the entry lives.
   * Passing `0` or omitting `ttl` stores the entry indefinitely.
   *
   * @param {string} key - The key to store the value under.
   * @param {unknown} value - The value to cache. Can be any type.
   * @param {number} [ttl=0] - Time-to-live in milliseconds. `0` means no expiry.
   * @returns {this} The cache instance, enabling method chaining.
   *
   * @example
   * cache.set("theme", "dark");                  // no expiry
   * cache.set("session", "abc123", 60_000);      // expires in 60 seconds
   * cache.set("a", 1).set("b", 2).set("c", 3);  // chained
   */
  set(key, value, ttl = 0) {
    if (typeof key !== "string" || key.trim() === "") {
      throw new TypeError("Cache key must be a non-empty string.");
    }

    if (typeof ttl !== "number" || ttl < 0) {
      throw new TypeError("TTL must be a non-negative number.");
    }

    this.#store.set(key, {
      value,
      expiresAt: ttl > 0 ? Date.now() + ttl : null,
    });

    return this;
  }

  /**
   * Checks whether a key exists in the cache and has not expired.
   *
   * @param {string} key - The key to check.
   * @returns {boolean} `true` if the key exists and is still valid, `false` otherwise.
   *
   * @example
   * cache.set("ready", true);
   * cache.has("ready");   // true
   * cache.has("missing"); // false
   */
  has(key) {
    return this.get(key) !== undefined;
  }

  /**
   * Removes a key-value pair from the cache.
   * Has no effect if the key does not exist.
   *
   * @param {string} key - The key to remove.
   * @returns {boolean} `true` if the key existed and was removed, `false` otherwise.
   *
   * @example
   * cache.set("temp", 42);
   * cache.delete("temp");    // true
   * cache.delete("missing"); // false
   */
  delete(key) {
    return this.#store.delete(key);
  }
}
