## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Exponential backoff** | Prevents hammering an already-struggling server |
| **±10 % jitter** | Spreads retries across many clients (avoids thundering herd) |
| **`Retry-After` header** | Respects server-specified rate-limit windows (RFC 7231) |
| **`AbortSignal` threading** | Lets callers cancel mid-sleep or mid-fetch without leaking timers |
| **Custom `RetryError`** | Preserves the last error/response for caller inspection/logging |
| **No retry on 4xx (except listed)** | 400/401/403/404 are client errors — retrying won't help |
| **`onRetry` callback** | Decouples logging/metrics from retry logic |

# `fetchWithRetry` - Fetch with Exponential Backoff Retry

## Overview

A robust wrapper around the native `fetch` API that automatically retries failed requests using exponential backoff with jitter, preventing thundering herd problems.

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches a URL with automatic retry logic using exponential backoff.
 *
 * Retry behavior:
 *  - Retries on network errors (e.g., DNS failure, connection refused)
 *  - Retries on configurable HTTP status codes (default: 429, 500, 502, 503, 504)
 *  - Does NOT retry on client errors (4xx) unless explicitly included
 *  - Uses exponential backoff: delay = baseDelay * 2^attempt + random jitter
 *  - Respects `Retry-After` response header when present
 *
 * @param {string|URL}  url                          - The URL to fetch.
 * @param {RequestInit} [options={}]                 - Standard fetch options (method, headers, body, etc.)
 * @param {object}      [retryOptions={}]            - Retry configuration.
 * @param {number}      [retryOptions.retries=3]     - Maximum number of retry attempts (not counting the initial request).
 * @param {number}      [retryOptions.baseDelay=300] - Base delay in milliseconds before the first retry.
 * @param {number}      [retryOptions.maxDelay=30000]- Maximum delay in milliseconds between retries.
 * @param {number[]}    [retryOptions.retryOn=[429, 500, 502, 503, 504]]
 *                                                   - HTTP status codes that should trigger a retry.
 * @param {Function}    [retryOptions.onRetry]       - Optional callback invoked before each retry.
 *                                                     Receives ({ attempt, retries, delay, error, response }).
 * @param {AbortSignal} [retryOptions.signal]        - Optional AbortSignal to cancel all retries immediately.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<Response>} Resolves with the first successful Response.
 *
 * @throws {FetchRetryError} When all attempts are exhausted or the request is aborted.
 *                           The error exposes `.cause` (original error/response) and `.attempts`.
 *
 * @example
 * // Basic usage — 3 retries with defaults
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');
 * const data = await response.json();
 *
 * @example
 * // Custom retry config with progress logging
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/upload',
 *   { method: 'POST', body: formData },
 *   {
 *     retries: 5,
 *     baseDelay: 500,
 *     maxDelay: 15000,
 *     retryOn: [429, 500, 503],
 *     onRetry: ({ attempt, retries, delay, error, response }) => {
 *       const reason = error?.message ?? `HTTP ${response?.status}`;
 *       console.warn(`Attempt ${attempt}/${retries} failed (${reason}). Retrying in ${delay}ms…`);
 *     },
 *   }
 * );
 *
 * @example
 * // Abort all retries after a timeout
 * const controller = new AbortController();
 * setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10_000); // give up after 10 s total
 *
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/slow',
 *   {},
 *   { retries: 4, signal: controller.signal }
 * );
 */

// ─── Custom Error ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Error thrown when fetchWithRetry exhausts all attempts or is aborted.
 *
 * @property {number}         attempts - Total number of attempts made (initial + retries).
 * @property {Error|Response} cause    - The last error or non-retryable Response that caused failure.
 */
class FetchRetryError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string}         message
   * @param {number}         attempts
   * @param {Error|Response} cause
   */
  constructor(message, attempts, cause) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'FetchRetryError';
    this.attempts = attempts;
    this.cause = cause; // native ES2022 error cause
  }
}

// ─── Helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Returns a promise that resolves after `ms` milliseconds.
 * Rejects immediately if the provided AbortSignal fires during the wait.
 *
 * @param {number}       ms
 * @param {AbortSignal}  [signal]
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      return reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    }

    const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);

    signal?.addEventListener('abort', () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    }, { once: true });
  });
}

/**
 * Calculates the delay before the next retry attempt.
 * Formula: min(baseDelay * 2^attempt, maxDelay) + random jitter (±20 %)
 *
 * The jitter prevents multiple clients from retrying in lockstep
 * ("thundering herd" / "retry storm").
 *
 * @param {number} attempt    - Zero-based attempt index (0 = first retry).
 * @param {number} baseDelay  - Base delay in ms.
 * @param {number} maxDelay   - Upper cap in ms.
 * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds (always ≥ 0).
 */
function calcBackoffDelay(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay) {
  const exponential = baseDelay * Math.pow(2, attempt); // 300, 600, 1200, …
  const capped = Math.min(exponential, maxDelay);
  const jitter = capped * 0.2 * (Math.random() * 2 - 1); // ±20 %
  return Math.max(0, Math.round(capped + jitter));
}

/**
 * Parses the value of a `Retry-After` HTTP response header.
 * Supports both delta-seconds ("120") and HTTP-date ("Wed, 21 Oct 2025 07:28:00 GMT").
 *
 * @param {Response} response
 * @returns {number|null} Milliseconds to wait, or null if the header is absent/invalid.
 */
function parseRetryAfter(response) {
  const header = response.headers?.get('Retry-After');
  if (!header) return null;

  const seconds = Number(header);
  if (!Number.isNaN(seconds)) return seconds * 1000;

  const date = Date.parse(header);
  if (!Number.isNaN(date)) return Math.max(0, date - Date.now());

  return null;
}

// ─── Main Function ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

async function fetchWithRetry(
  url,
  options = {},
  {
    retries   = 3,
    baseDelay = 300,
    maxDelay  = 30_000,
    retryOn   = [429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
    onRetry   = null,
    signal    = null,
  } = {}
) {
  // Merge the caller's AbortSignal with our retry signal so both can cancel.
  const mergedOptions = signal ? { ...options, signal } : options;

  let lastError;
  let lastResponse;

  // attempt 0 = initial request, attempts 1..retries = retries
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    // ── Abort check before every attempt ──────────────────────────────────
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      throw new FetchRetryError(
        'fetchWithRetry aborted before attempt ' + attempt,
        attempt,
        new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError')
      );
    }

    try {
      const response = await fetch(url, mergedOptions);

      // ── Success path ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      if (!retryOn.includes(response.status)) {
        return response; // ← happy path
      }

      // ── Retryable HTTP status ─────────────────────────────────────────────
      lastResponse = response;
      lastError = null;

      // If this was our last attempt, stop here (don't sleep pointlessly).
      if (attempt === retries) break;

      // Prefer server-specified back-off, fall back to exponential calculation.
      const retryAfterMs = parseRetryAfter(response);
      const delay = retryAfterMs ?? calcBackoffDelay(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay);

      onRetry?.({ attempt: attempt + 1, retries, delay, error: null, response });
      await sleep(delay, signal);

    } catch (err) {
      // ── Network / abort error ─────────────────────────────────────────────
      // Re-throw immediately on abort — no point retrying.
      if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
        throw new FetchRetryError(
          'fetchWithRetry was aborted',
          attempt + 1,
          err
        );
      }

      lastError = err;
      lastResponse = null;

      if (attempt === retries) break;

      const delay = calcBackoffDelay(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay);
      onRetry?.({ attempt: attempt + 1, retries, delay, error: err, response: null });
      await sleep(delay, signal);
    }
  }

  // ── All attempts exhausted ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const totalAttempts = retries + 1;
  const cause = lastError ?? lastResponse;
  const reason = lastError
    ? lastError.message
    : `HTTP ${lastResponse?.status}`;

  throw new FetchRetryError(
    `fetchWithRetry failed after ${totalAttempts} attempt(s): ${reason}`,
    totalAttempts,
    cause
  );
}
```

---

## Usage Examples

```javascript
// ── 1. Drop-in replacement for fetch ─────────────────────────────────────────
const res  = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/users');
const data = await res.json();


// ── 2. POST with a custom body ────────────────────────────────────────────────
const res = await fetchWithRetry(
  'https://api.example.com/orders',
  {
    method:  'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body:    JSON.stringify({ item: 'book', qty: 2 }),
  },
  { retries: 4, baseDelay: 500 }
);


// ── 3. Verbose retry logging ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
const res = await fetchWithRetry(
  'https://api.example.com/data',
  {},
  {
    retries: 5,
    onRetry: ({ attempt, retries, delay, error, response }) => {
      const reason = error?.message ?? `HTTP ${response?.status}`;
      console.warn(`[retry ${attempt}/${retries}] ${reason} — waiting ${delay} ms`);
    },
  }
);


// ── 4. Hard time-box with AbortController ────────────────────────────────────
const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10_000); // 10 s total budget

try {
  const res = await fetchWithRetry(
    'https://api.example.com/slow-endpoint',
    {},
    { retries: 6, signal: controller.signal }
  );
} catch (err) {
  if (err.cause?.name === 'AbortError') console.error('Timed out!');
  else throw err;
}


// ── 5. Structured error handling ──────────────────────────────────────────────
try {
  const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/flaky');
  return await res.json();
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof FetchRetryError) {
    console.error(`Failed after ${err.attempts} attempts.`, err.cause);
    // err.cause is the last Error (network) or Response (bad status)
  }
  throw err;
}
```

---

## How the Backoff Works

| Attempt | Formula (`baseDelay=300`) | Approx. delay (before jitter) |
|---------|--------------------------|-------------------------------|
| 1st retry | 300 × 2⁰ = 300 ms | ~240 – 360 ms |
| 2nd retry | 300 × 2¹ = 600 ms | ~480 – 720 ms |
| 3rd retry | 300 × 2² = 1 200 ms | ~960 ms – 1.44 s |
| 4th retry | 300 × 2³ = 2 400 ms | ~1.92 – 2.88 s |
| … | … (capped at `maxDelay`) | … |

> **Jitter** adds ±20 % randomness so that multiple clients retrying simultaneously don't all hammer the server at the exact same millisecond.

## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|----------|-----------|
| **Jitter on backoff** | Prevents retry storms when many clients fail simultaneously |
| **`Retry-After` header respected** | Servers that rate-limit (HTTP 429) often tell you exactly how long to wait |
| **AbortError is never retried** | Abort is intentional — retrying would ignore the caller's intent |
| **`FetchRetryError` wraps the cause** | Callers can inspect the original error or the bad Response without losing context |
| **`onRetry` callback** | Keeps logging/metrics out of the core function (single responsibility) |

# `fetchWithRetry` — Fetch with Exponential Backoff Retry

## Overview

A robust wrapper around the native `fetch` API that automatically retries failed requests using exponential backoff with optional jitter, giving transient network issues time to resolve before giving up.

---

## Implementation

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches a URL with automatic retry logic using exponential backoff.
 *
 * Retry behavior:
 *  - Retries on network errors (e.g., DNS failure, connection refused).
 *  - Retries on configurable HTTP status codes (default: 429, 500, 502, 503, 504).
 *  - Does NOT retry on client errors (4xx) unless explicitly included in `retryOn`.
 *  - Delay between retries doubles each attempt (exponential backoff).
 *  - Optional random jitter prevents thundering-herd problems.
 *
 * @param {string|URL} url
 *   The resource URL to fetch.
 *
 * @param {RequestInit} [options={}]
 *   Standard fetch options (method, headers, body, signal, etc.).
 *   If `options.signal` is an AbortSignal, retries stop immediately on abort.
 *
 * @param {object} [retryOptions={}]
 *   Configuration for retry behaviour.
 *
 * @param {number} [retryOptions.retries=3]
 *   Maximum number of retry attempts after the initial request.
 *   Total attempts = retries + 1.
 *
 * @param {number} [retryOptions.baseDelay=300]
 *   Initial delay in milliseconds before the first retry.
 *   Subsequent delays: baseDelay * 2^attemptIndex.
 *
 * @param {number} [retryOptions.maxDelay=10000]
 *   Upper cap on delay (ms) to prevent excessively long waits.
 *
 * @param {boolean} [retryOptions.jitter=true]
 *   When true, adds a random component (0–50% of computed delay) to each
 *   delay to spread retries from concurrent callers.
 *
 * @param {number[]} [retryOptions.retryOn=[429, 500, 502, 503, 504]]
 *   HTTP status codes that should trigger a retry.
 *   All network-level errors are always retried regardless of this list.
 *
 * @param {function(number, Error|Response): void} [retryOptions.onRetry]
 *   Optional callback invoked before each retry.
 *   Receives (attemptNumber, errorOrResponse) — useful for logging/metrics.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<Response>}
 *   Resolves with the first successful `Response` object.
 *
 * @throws {FetchRetryError}
 *   Thrown after all attempts are exhausted, or immediately if the request
 *   is aborted. Wraps the last error/response for inspection.
 *
 * @example
 * // Basic usage
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');
 * const data = await response.json();
 *
 * @example
 * // POST with custom retry settings and logging
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/submit',
 *   {
 *     method: 'POST',
 *     headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
 *     body: JSON.stringify({ key: 'value' }),
 *   },
 *   {
 *     retries: 5,
 *     baseDelay: 500,
 *     maxDelay: 15000,
 *     retryOn: [429, 500, 503],
 *     onRetry: (attempt, err) => {
 *       console.warn(`Retry #${attempt}`, err?.status ?? err?.message);
 *     },
 *   }
 * );
 *
 * @example
 * // Abort after 8 seconds regardless of retries
 * const controller = new AbortController();
 * setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 8000);
 *
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/slow',
 *   { signal: controller.signal },
 *   { retries: 10, baseDelay: 1000 }
 * );
 */

// ─── Custom Error ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Error thrown by fetchWithRetry when all attempts have been exhausted
 * or the request is aborted.
 *
 * @property {number}          attempts  - Total attempts made (initial + retries).
 * @property {Error|Response}  cause     - The last error or non-OK Response.
 */
class FetchRetryError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string}           message
   * @param {number}           attempts
   * @param {Error|Response}   cause
   */
  constructor(message, attempts, cause) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'FetchRetryError';
    this.attempts = attempts;
    this.cause = cause; // native Error.cause (also supported by older runtimes via this property)
  }
}

// ─── Helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Returns a promise that resolves after `ms` milliseconds.
 * Rejects immediately if the optional AbortSignal fires during the wait.
 *
 * @param {number}       ms
 * @param {AbortSignal}  [signal]
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      return reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    }

    const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);

    signal?.addEventListener(
      'abort',
      () => {
        clearTimeout(timer);
        reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
      },
      { once: true },
    );
  });
}

/**
 * Computes the delay for a given attempt using capped exponential backoff
 * and optional jitter.
 *
 * Formula (no jitter):  min(baseDelay * 2^attempt, maxDelay)
 * Formula (jitter):     delay + random(0, delay * 0.5)
 *
 * @param {number}  attempt    - Zero-based retry index.
 * @param {number}  baseDelay  - Starting delay in ms.
 * @param {number}  maxDelay   - Maximum delay in ms.
 * @param {boolean} jitter     - Whether to add random jitter.
 * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds.
 */
function computeDelay(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay, jitter) {
  const exponential = Math.min(baseDelay * 2 ** attempt, maxDelay);
  return jitter
    ? exponential + Math.random() * exponential * 0.5
    : exponential;
}

// ─── Core Function ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}, retryOptions = {}) {
  const {
    retries   = 3,
    baseDelay = 300,
    maxDelay  = 10_000,
    jitter    = true,
    retryOn   = [429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
    onRetry   = null,
  } = retryOptions;

  const { signal } = options;
  const retrySet   = new Set(retryOn);

  let lastError;

  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    // ── Abort guard before every attempt ──────────────────────────────────
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      throw new FetchRetryError(
        `Request aborted before attempt ${attempt + 1}.`,
        attempt,
        new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'),
      );
    }

    try {
      const response = await fetch(url, options);

      // ── Success path ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
      if (response.ok || !retrySet.has(response.status)) {
        /*
         * Return the response when:
         *   a) HTTP 2xx — clear success.
         *   b) Status not in retryOn list — caller must handle it
         *      (e.g. 404 Not Found is a definitive answer, not a transient error).
         */
        return response;
      }

      // ── Retryable HTTP error ───────────────────────────────────────────
      lastError = response;

    } catch (err) {
      // ── Abort should not be retried ────────────────────────────────────
      if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
        throw new FetchRetryError(
          'Request was aborted.',
          attempt + 1,
          err,
        );
      }

      // ── Network / other transient error ───────────────────────────────
      lastError = err;
    }

    // ── No more retries left ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    if (attempt === retries) break;

    // ── Notify caller before waiting ──────────────────────────────────────
    onRetry?.(attempt + 1, lastError);

    // ── Wait before next attempt ──────────────────────────────────────────
    const delay = computeDelay(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay, jitter);
    await sleep(delay, signal);
  }

  // ── All attempts exhausted ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  const totalAttempts = retries + 1;
  const reason =
    lastError instanceof Response
      ? `HTTP ${lastError.status} after ${totalAttempts} attempt(s).`
      : `Network error after ${totalAttempts} attempt(s): ${lastError?.message}`;

  throw new FetchRetryError(reason, totalAttempts, lastError);
}
```

---

## Behavior at a Glance

| Scenario | Behavior |
|---|---|
| HTTP 2xx | ✅ Returns `Response` immediately |
| HTTP 4xx (not in `retryOn`) | ✅ Returns `Response` — caller decides |
| HTTP 429 / 5xx (in `retryOn`) | 🔁 Retried with backoff |
| Network error (DNS, timeout…) | 🔁 Always retried |
| `AbortError` | 🛑 Throws immediately, no retry |
| All retries exhausted | 💥 Throws `FetchRetryError` |

---

## Delay Schedule (defaults: `baseDelay=300`, `maxDelay=10000`, `jitter=false`)

| Retry # | Formula | Delay |
|---|---|---|
| 1st retry | 300 × 2⁰ | ~300 ms |
| 2nd retry | 300 × 2¹ | ~600 ms |
| 3rd retry | 300 × 2² | ~1 200 ms |
| 4th retry | 300 × 2³ | ~2 400 ms |
| 5th retry | 300 × 2⁴ | ~4 800 ms |
| 6th+ retry | capped | 10 000 ms |

With **jitter enabled** (default), each value gains a random `+0–50%` offset.

---

## Error Handling

```javascript
import { fetchWithRetry, FetchRetryError } from './fetchWithRetry.js';

try {
  const res = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data', {}, {
    retries: 4,
    onRetry: (n, cause) => console.warn(`Attempt ${n} failed:`, cause),
  });

  if (!res.ok) {
    // Definitive non-retryable error (e.g. 404, 403)
    throw new Error(`Unexpected status: ${res.status}`);
  }

  const data = await res.json();
  console.log(data);

} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof FetchRetryError) {
    console.error(`All ${err.attempts} attempt(s) failed.`);
    console.error('Last cause:', err.cause);
  } else {
    throw err; // re-throw unexpected errors
  }
}
```

---

## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Separate `retryOptions` object** | Keeps `options` compatible with the native `fetch` API signature |
| **`retryOn` whitelist** | Avoids silently swallowing definitive errors (401, 403, 404) |
| **`sleep` respects `AbortSignal`** | Cancellation works even during the backoff wait, not just during fetch |
| **`FetchRetryError` wraps last cause** | Caller can inspect the original error/response without losing context |
| **Jitter on by default** | Prevents thundering-herd when many clients retry simultaneously |

# `fetchWithRetry` — Fetch with Exponential Backoff Retry

## Overview

A robust wrapper around the native `fetch` API that automatically retries failed requests using exponential backoff with jitter, making it resilient to transient network failures and temporary server errors.

---

## Implementation

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches a URL with automatic retry logic using exponential backoff.
 *
 * Retry behavior:
 * - Retries on network errors (e.g., DNS failure, connection refused)
 * - Retries on configurable HTTP status codes (default: 429, 500, 502, 503, 504)
 * - Does NOT retry on client errors (4xx) unless explicitly included in retryOn
 * - Uses exponential backoff: delay = baseDelay * 2^attempt + random jitter
 * - Throws the last encountered error/response after all retries are exhausted
 *
 * @param {string | URL | Request} url
 *   The resource URL to fetch. Accepts the same types as native fetch().
 *
 * @param {RequestInit & FetchWithRetryOptions} [options={}]
 *   Standard fetch() options extended with retry-specific configuration.
 *
 * @param {number} [options.retries=3]
 *   Maximum number of retry attempts after the initial request.
 *   Total attempts = retries + 1. Must be >= 0.
 *
 * @param {number} [options.baseDelay=300]
 *   Base delay in milliseconds before the first retry.
 *   Subsequent delays follow: baseDelay * 2^attemptIndex.
 *
 * @param {number} [options.maxDelay=30000]
 *   Maximum delay cap in milliseconds to prevent excessively long waits.
 *
 * @param {number[]} [options.retryOn=[429, 500, 502, 503, 504]]
 *   HTTP status codes that should trigger a retry.
 *   Non-listed status codes (including other 4xx) will NOT be retried.
 *
 * @param {function(attempt: number, error: Error|null, response: Response|null): boolean} [options.retryCondition]
 *   Optional custom function to determine whether a retry should occur.
 *   Overrides the default retryOn logic when provided.
 *   Receives:
 *     - attempt {number}       Current attempt index (0-based)
 *     - error   {Error|null}   Network error, or null if a response was received
 *     - response{Response|null}HTTP response, or null if a network error occurred
 *   Returns {boolean} true to retry, false to stop and throw.
 *
 * @param {function(attempt: number, delay: number): void} [options.onRetry]
 *   Optional callback invoked before each retry.
 *   Useful for logging, metrics, or custom side effects.
 *   Receives:
 *     - attempt {number} The retry attempt number (1-based)
 *     - delay   {number} The computed delay in milliseconds before this retry
 *
 * @param {AbortSignal} [options.signal]
 *   Standard AbortSignal. Retries are cancelled immediately if the signal fires.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<Response>}
 *   Resolves with the first successful Response object.
 *   "Successful" means: response.ok === true, OR a non-retryable status code.
 *
 * @throws {Error} On network failure after all retries are exhausted.
 * @throws {Response} On HTTP error response after all retries are exhausted.
 *   Check the thrown value's `status` property to distinguish from network errors.
 * @throws {DOMException} With name "AbortError" if the request is aborted.
 *
 * @example
 * // Basic usage — 3 retries with default settings
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');
 * const data = await response.json();
 *
 * @example
 * // POST request with custom retry count and base delay
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/submit',
 *   {
 *     method: 'POST',
 *     headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
 *     body: JSON.stringify({ key: 'value' }),
 *     retries: 5,
 *     baseDelay: 500,
 *     maxDelay: 10000,
 *   }
 * );
 *
 * @example
 * // Custom retry condition (retry only on 503)
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(url, {
 *   retryCondition: (attempt, error, response) => {
 *     if (error) return true; // always retry network errors
 *     return response?.status === 503;
 *   },
 * });
 *
 * @example
 * // With abort controller and retry logging
 * const controller = new AbortController();
 * setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 10000); // 10s global timeout
 *
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(url, {
 *   signal: controller.signal,
 *   onRetry: (attempt, delay) => {
 *     console.warn(`Retry #${attempt} in ${delay}ms`);
 *   },
 * });
 */
async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}) {
  const {
    retries = 3,
    baseDelay = 300,
    maxDelay = 30_000,
    retryOn = [429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
    retryCondition = null,
    onRetry = null,
    // Remaining keys are passed directly to fetch()
    ...fetchOptions
  } = options;

  // ─── Input Validation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  if (!Number.isInteger(retries) || retries < 0) {
    throw new TypeError(`fetchWithRetry: "retries" must be a non-negative integer, got ${retries}`);
  }
  if (typeof baseDelay !== 'number' || baseDelay < 0) {
    throw new TypeError(`fetchWithRetry: "baseDelay" must be a non-negative number, got ${baseDelay}`);
  }
  if (typeof maxDelay !== 'number' || maxDelay < baseDelay) {
    throw new TypeError(`fetchWithRetry: "maxDelay" must be >= baseDelay, got ${maxDelay}`);
  }

  // ─── Helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  /**
   * Computes the delay for a given attempt using full-jitter exponential backoff.
   *
   * Formula: random(0, min(maxDelay, baseDelay * 2^attempt))
   * Full jitter prevents thundering herd when many clients retry simultaneously.
   *
   * @param {number} attempt - Zero-based attempt index
   * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds
   */
  const computeDelay = (attempt) => {
    const exponential = Math.min(maxDelay, baseDelay * Math.pow(2, attempt));
    return Math.floor(Math.random() * exponential); // full jitter
  };

  /**
   * Returns a promise that resolves after `ms` milliseconds,
   * but rejects immediately if the AbortSignal fires.
   *
   * @param {number} ms
   * @param {AbortSignal|undefined} signal
   * @returns {Promise<void>}
   */
  const sleep = (ms, signal) =>
    new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      if (signal?.aborted) {
        return reject(new DOMException('The operation was aborted.', 'AbortError'));
      }

      const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);

      signal?.addEventListener('abort', () => {
        clearTimeout(timer);
        reject(new DOMException('The operation was aborted.', 'AbortError'));
      }, { once: true });
    });

  /**
   * Determines whether a retry should be attempted.
   *
   * @param {number} attempt
   * @param {Error|null} error
   * @param {Response|null} response
   * @returns {boolean}
   */
  const shouldRetry = (attempt, error, response) => {
    if (attempt >= retries) return false;

    // Abort errors should never be retried
    if (error?.name === 'AbortError') return false;

    // Delegate to custom condition if provided
    if (retryCondition) return retryCondition(attempt, error, response);

    // Default: retry network errors and configured status codes
    if (error) return true;
    return retryOn.includes(response?.status);
  };

  // ─── Main Retry Loop ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  let lastError = null;
  let lastResponse = null;

  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url, fetchOptions);

      // Successful response — return immediately
      if (response.ok || !shouldRetry(attempt, null, response)) {
        return response;
      }

      // Retryable HTTP error — store and continue loop
      lastResponse = response;
      lastError = null;

    } catch (error) {
      // AbortError — stop immediately, do not retry
      if (error.name === 'AbortError') throw error;

      // Network error — store and maybe retry
      lastError = error;
      lastResponse = null;

      if (!shouldRetry(attempt, error, null)) throw error;
    }

    // If we reach here, a retry is warranted
    const delay = computeDelay(attempt);
    onRetry?.(attempt + 1, delay);
    await sleep(delay, fetchOptions.signal);
  }

  // All retries exhausted — throw the most recent failure
  if (lastError) throw lastError;
  throw lastResponse; // HTTP error Response object
}
```

---

## Behavior Reference Table

| Scenario | Default Behavior | Customizable Via |
|---|---|---|
| Network error (DNS, timeout) | ✅ Retried | `retryCondition` |
| `500`, `502`, `503`, `504` | ✅ Retried | `retryOn`, `retryCondition` |
| `429 Too Many Requests` | ✅ Retried | `retryOn`, `retryCondition` |
| `400`, `401`, `403`, `404` | ❌ Not retried | `retryOn`, `retryCondition` |
| `AbortError` | ❌ Never retried | — (hard rule) |
| Successful `2xx` | ✅ Returned immediately | — |

---

## Delay Schedule (Default: `baseDelay=300ms`)

```
Attempt 1 → random(0,  300ms)   ≈  0–300ms
Attempt 2 → random(0,  600ms)   ≈  0–600ms
Attempt 3 → random(0, 1200ms)   ≈  0–1200ms
Attempt 4 → random(0, 2400ms)   ≈  0–2400ms
...capped at maxDelay (30,000ms)
```

Full jitter is used to spread retry load across distributed clients.

---

## Error Handling Pattern

```javascript
try {
  const response = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data', {
    retries: 3,
    onRetry: (attempt, delay) =>
      console.warn(`Retry #${attempt} after ${delay}ms`),
  });

  const data = await response.json();
  console.log(data);

} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof Response) {
    // HTTP error after all retries (e.g., persistent 503)
    console.error(`HTTP ${err.status}: ${err.statusText}`);
  } else if (err.name === 'AbortError') {
    console.error('Request was cancelled');
  } else {
    // Network failure after all retries
    console.error('Network error:', err.message);
  }
}
```

---

## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **Full jitter** over fixed backoff | Prevents [thundering herd](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/architecture/exponential-backoff-and-jitter/) when many clients retry at once |
| **Throws `Response`** on HTTP error | Preserves full response metadata (status, headers, body) for the caller |
| **Never retries `AbortError`** | Respecting abort is a contract — retrying would violate caller intent |
| **`retryCondition` overrides `retryOn`** | Gives callers full control without forking the function |
| **`sleep` respects `AbortSignal`** | Ensures abort works even during the wait period between retries |

# fetchWithRetry - Fetch with Exponential Backoff

## Overview

A robust fetch wrapper that automatically retries failed requests using exponential backoff with jitter, providing resilience against transient network failures and temporary server errors.

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches a URL with automatic retry logic using exponential backoff.
 *
 * Retry behavior:
 * - Retries on network errors (no response received)
 * - Retries on configurable HTTP status codes (default: 429, 500, 502, 503, 504)
 * - Does NOT retry on client errors (4xx) unless explicitly configured
 * - Uses exponential backoff: delay = baseDelay * 2^attempt + random jitter
 *
 * @param {string} url
 *   The URL to fetch.
 *
 * @param {Object} [options={}]
 *   Standard fetch options (method, headers, body, etc.) plus retry config.
 *
 * @param {number} [options.retries=3]
 *   Maximum number of retry attempts after the initial request.
 *
 * @param {number} [options.baseDelay=300]
 *   Base delay in milliseconds before the first retry.
 *   Subsequent retries double this value (exponential backoff).
 *
 * @param {number} [options.maxDelay=10000]
 *   Maximum delay cap in milliseconds to prevent excessively long waits.
 *
 * @param {number[]} [options.retryOn=[429, 500, 502, 503, 504]]
 *   HTTP status codes that should trigger a retry.
 *
 * @param {Function} [options.onRetry]
 *   Optional callback invoked before each retry attempt.
 *   Receives ({ attempt, retries, delay, error, response }) as argument.
 *
 * @param {AbortSignal} [options.signal]
 *   Optional AbortSignal to cancel the request and all pending retries.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<Response>}
 *   Resolves with the fetch Response on success.
 *
 * @throws {FetchRetryError}
 *   Throws after all retries are exhausted. Includes the last error and
 *   a history of all attempts for debugging.
 *
 * @example
 * // Basic usage
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry('https://api.example.com/data');
 * const data = await response.json();
 *
 * @example
 * // Custom retry configuration
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/data',
 *   {
 *     method: 'POST',
 *     headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
 *     body: JSON.stringify({ key: 'value' }),
 *     retries: 5,
 *     baseDelay: 500,
 *     maxDelay: 15000,
 *     retryOn: [429, 500, 503],
 *     onRetry: ({ attempt, retries, delay, error, response }) => {
 *       console.warn(
 *         `Retry ${attempt}/${retries} in ${delay}ms`,
 *         error ?? `HTTP ${response.status}`
 *       );
 *     },
 *   }
 * );
 *
 * @example
 * // With abort/timeout support
 * const controller = new AbortController();
 * setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 8000); // Global 8s timeout
 *
 * const response = await fetchWithRetry(
 *   'https://api.example.com/data',
 *   { signal: controller.signal, retries: 4 }
 * );
 */

// ─── Custom Error Class ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Error thrown when all retry attempts are exhausted.
 * Extends the native Error with additional retry context.
 */
class FetchRetryError extends Error {
  /**
   * @param {string} message - Human-readable error description.
   * @param {Object} context - Retry context for debugging.
   * @param {string} context.url - The URL that was requested.
   * @param {Error|null} context.lastError - The last network/abort error, if any.
   * @param {Response|null} context.lastResponse - The last HTTP response, if any.
   * @param {Array<Object>} context.attempts - History of all attempts.
   */
  constructor(message, { url, lastError, lastResponse, attempts }) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'FetchRetryError';
    this.url = url;
    this.lastError = lastError;
    this.lastResponse = lastResponse;
    this.attempts = attempts; // Full history for debugging
  }
}

// ─── Helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * Calculates the delay before the next retry using exponential backoff
 * with full jitter to spread out retry storms across multiple clients.
 *
 * Formula: random(0, min(maxDelay, baseDelay * 2^attempt))
 *
 * @param {number} attempt - Zero-based attempt index (0 = first retry).
 * @param {number} baseDelay - Base delay in milliseconds.
 * @param {number} maxDelay - Maximum delay cap in milliseconds.
 * @returns {number} Delay in milliseconds.
 */
function calculateBackoff(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay) {
  const exponential = baseDelay * Math.pow(2, attempt); // e.g. 300, 600, 1200…
  const capped = Math.min(exponential, maxDelay);        // respect the ceiling
  return Math.floor(Math.random() * capped);             // full jitter
}

/**
 * Returns a Promise that resolves after `ms` milliseconds,
 * but rejects immediately if the provided AbortSignal fires.
 *
 * @param {number} ms - Milliseconds to wait.
 * @param {AbortSignal|null} signal - Optional signal to cancel the wait.
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
function sleep(ms, signal = null) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal?.aborted) {
      return reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    }

    const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);

    signal?.addEventListener('abort', () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    }, { once: true });
  });
}

// ─── Core Function ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

async function fetchWithRetry(url, options = {}) {
  const {
    retries   = 3,
    baseDelay = 300,
    maxDelay  = 10_000,
    retryOn   = [429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
    onRetry   = null,
    signal    = null,
    ...fetchOptions               // remaining keys are passed to fetch()
  } = options;

  const attempts = []; // audit trail of every attempt
  let lastError    = null;
  let lastResponse = null;

  // Total iterations = initial request + up to `retries` retries
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    const isRetry = attempt > 0;

    try {
      // ── Make the request ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
      const response = await fetch(url, { ...fetchOptions, signal });
      lastResponse = response;

      attempts.push({
        attempt,
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
        status: response.status,
        ok: response.ok,
      });

      // ── Success ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      if (!retryOn.includes(response.status)) {
        return response; // Caller handles non-retryable 4xx errors
      }

      // ── Retryable HTTP status ─────────────────────────────────────────────
      if (attempt < retries) {
        const delay = calculateBackoff(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay);

        onRetry?.({ attempt: attempt + 1, retries, delay, error: null, response });

        await sleep(delay, signal);
        continue; // next iteration
      }

      // Exhausted all retries on a bad status code
      throw new FetchRetryError(
        `Request to ${url} failed with HTTP ${response.status} after ${retries + 1} attempt(s).`,
        { url, lastError: null, lastResponse: response, attempts }
      );

    } catch (error) {
      // ── Never retry abort errors ──────────────────────────────────────────
      if (error.name === 'AbortError') throw error;

      // ── Re-throw our own terminal error ───────────────────────────────────
      if (error instanceof FetchRetryError) throw error;

      // ── Network / unexpected error ────────────────────────────────────────
      lastError = error;

      attempts.push({
        attempt,
        timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
        status: null,
        ok: false,
        error: error.message,
      });

      if (attempt < retries) {
        const delay = calculateBackoff(attempt, baseDelay, maxDelay);

        onRetry?.({ attempt: attempt + 1, retries, delay, error, response: null });

        await sleep(delay, signal);
        continue; // next iteration
      }

      // Exhausted all retries on a network error
      throw new FetchRetryError(
        `Request to ${url} failed after ${retries + 1} attempt(s): ${error.message}`,
        { url, lastError: error, lastResponse, attempts }
      );
    }
  }
}
```

